Mild External Hemorrhoids
Common Infections and Afflictions
Anal verge lump, Bright red blood on wiping, Perianal pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-027
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of bright red rectal bleeding and a perianal lump.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting sudden bright red blood on toilet paper accompanied by a newly palpable mass at the anal verge. The acute onset of rectal bleeding combined with an anatomical lump generates immense patient distress and clinical concern for active lower gastrointestinal hemorrhage, an acutely thrombosed external hemorrhoid requiring emergent incision, or prolapsed rectal tissue. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform direct visual and digital rectal examination to accurately localize the bleeding source and definitively rule out surgical emergencies. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of missing a rapidly progressing thrombosed hemorrhoid or escalating patient anxiety regarding malignancy, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I saw bright red blood on the paper when I wiped, and there is a very itchy, uncomfortable lump right on the outside.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 48 hours ago following an episode of difficult, straining defecation secondary to a chronically low-fiber diet. The patient reports the sudden appearance of a mildly uncomfortable lump exactly at the anal verge. Over the past 24 hours, the patient has noted streaks of bright red blood strictly confined to the toilet paper after bowel movements, alongside moderate perianal pruritus. The patient describes a persistent sensation of incomplete evacuation and notes the discomfort is acutely exacerbated by prolonged sitting. Denies severe, excruciating lancinating pain (which would suggest a fissure or severe thrombosis), dark tarry stools, abdominal pain, or unintentional weight loss.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently avoiding sitting flat due to localized discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute psychological distress triggered by the sight of rectal bleeding.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 130/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly anxious regarding the symptom presentation.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A normal anoscopy of the internal anal canal excluding internal hemorrhoids would assist in confirming the diagnosis in an outpatient setting, but is not acutely indicated over external visual inspection.
Physical Exam: Visual inspection of the perianal region reveals a 1cm, visible bluish-purple nodule covered by squamous epithelium located at the anal verge. The mass is soft, easily palpable, and mildly tender to touch but lacks the tense, firm, exquisitely painful characteristics of an acute thrombosis. No mucosal prolapse from the rectum is visible during simulated valsalva maneuver. No frank active bleeding or anal fissures are observed. Digital rectal examination demonstrates normal resting sphincter tone, an empty rectal vault, and no palpable masses or internal irregularities. No macroscopic blood on the examining glove.
Environmental Observations: Residence is secure. Bathroom lacks a footstool for proper defecation posturing.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has not applied any topical preparations.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mild External Hemorrhoids - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Thrombosed External Hemorrhoid - Ruled out clinically as the visible nodule is soft and only mildly tender, rather than presenting as a tense, hard, dark purple, and excruciatingly painful mass requiring surgical evacuation.
Anal Fissure - Unlikely given the lack of severe, tearing pain during defecation and the direct visualization of the external hemorrhoidal nodule as the source of bleeding.
Colorectal Malignancy - Ruled out as the primary acute cause of this specific bleeding episode by identifying a clear, benign local source; however, age-appropriate routine screening remains indicated.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with acute, highly distressing rectal bleeding and a palpable mass, mandating immediate clinical assessment to differentiate benign anorectal pathology from surgical emergencies like a thrombosed hemorrhoid. Urgent on-site physical and digital rectal examination rapidly localized the bleeding to an uncomplicated, mild external hemorrhoid. By definitively ruling out serious lower gastrointestinal hemorrhage and providing immediate reassurance and targeted topical therapy, an unnecessary and highly distressing emergency department transport was successfully averted. This critical diagnostic differentiation and acute stabilization firmly satisfy the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a topical corticosteroid/local anesthetic preparation (e.g., Proctosedyl ointment) to be applied to the perianal area strictly twice daily for a maximum of 7 days to reduce localized pruritus and inflammation. Advised initiation of a bulk-forming laxative (e.g., Psyllium husk) 1 tablespoon daily with ample water to normalize stool consistency.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently eliminate the mechanical strain that causes these blood vessels to swell, place a small step stool under your feet while sitting on the toilet to elevate your knees above your hips. This specific posture mechanically straightens the rectum, allowing gravity to assist the bowel movement and completely eliminating the need to push or strain.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: Bathroom footstool recommended.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 2-3 weeks to ensure resolution of bleeding and to discuss routine bowel cancer screening protocols.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the lump becomes hard, dark purple, and exquisitely painful, if you begin passing large amounts of blood or dark tarry stools, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent or persistent rectal bleeding always warrants formal endoscopic investigation.
Clinical Course: The acute pruritus and bleeding streaks should resolve within 3 to 5 days of utilizing the medicated ointment and removing the mechanical strain of constipation. The external skin tag may persist permanently but will become asymptomatic.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the bleeding becomes continuous and drips into the toilet bowl, rather than just appearing on the toilet paper.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: You have a swollen blood vessel just under the skin at the exit of your bowel, caused by the physical pressure of straining. The prescribed ointment acts as a localized numbing agent and fire extinguisher for the itch.
Actionable Steps: Strictly limit your time sitting on the toilet to 3 minutes maximum; reading or scrolling on a phone while sitting mechanically forces blood to pool in the perianal area, exacerbating the swelling. Utilize flushable wet wipes or a bidet rather than dry toilet paper to avoid physically tearing the fragile skin over the lump.

SMS INFORMATION
